{"name":"Liquid Gold","hex":"#fdc675","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"liquid gold","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A kind of liquid preparation used to decorate ceramic ware with gold or gold colour.","Any liquid that is considered highly valuable, such as crude oil or breast milk:\nSandalwood oil\nCrude oil.\nWater.\nColostrum."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/liquid_gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Liquid Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Liquid Gold was an English disco group, from Brackley in Northamptonshire. Their biggest success came in 1980 with \"Dance Yourself Dizzy\", which peaked at number two on the UK chart."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquid_Gold","wikidata":"Q16962751"},{"title":"Liquid","blocks":[{"text":"Liquid is a state of matter with a definite volume but no fixed shape. When confined in a container and subject to a force such as gravity, liquids will adapt to the internal shape of the container in the direction of the force. Liquids are nearly incompressible, maintaining their volume even under pressure. The density of a liquid is usually close to that of a solid, and much higher than that of a gas. Liquids are a form of condensed matter alongside solids, and a form of fluid alongside gases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/330px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquid","wikidata":"Q11435"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Cavitating-prop.jpg/500px-Cavitating-prop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Cavitating-prop.jpg/500px-Cavitating-prop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cavitation in water from a boat propeller","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACavitating-prop.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg/500px-2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg/1280px-2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg/500px-2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Surface waves in water","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Viscosities.gif/500px-Viscosities.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm